<!DOCTYPE html>
{#
    Whether this page is dressed as a pre-account screen — see the branch
    further down. Resolved once, here, and deliberately not with the `default`
    filter, which replaces anything *falsy*: run through it, the `false` the
    one page that opts out passes in comes back as `true`, and the page opts
    silently back in.
#}
{% set auth_chrome = auth_chrome is not defined or auth_chrome %}
{#
    Two writers for one attribute, and only one of them per request.

    A signed-in page uses the account's own setting, which follows the reader
    from machine to machine. A signed-out one uses `guest_theme`, which
    InstanceContextMiddleware resolves from the cookie the sign-in screen's
    switch writes — the account wins wherever there is an account, and the
    palette is decided before the page paints either way.

    The palette is the account's too, resolved through the `Palette` enum so
    only one of its five keys can ever be printed here. A signed-out page wears
    the default, navy; its theme still follows the sign-in screen's switch.
    Both attributes are in the HTML that arrives, and the generated stylesheet
    covers every combination of them — including "system" through a media
    query — so the right colours are on first paint with no script at all.

    Density has only the one writer, and says so in the same shape rather than
    leaning on a null reader resolving to the same default by accident.

    `data-icon-sprite` is the hashed URL of the icon sprite, for the one script
    that builds an icon of its own after the page has loaded.
#}
<html lang="{{ locale_tag() }}"
      data-theme="{{ current_user ? current_user.themePreference().value : guest_theme|default('system') }}"
      data-palette="{{ current_user ? current_user.palettePreference().value : 'navy' }}"
      data-density="{{ current_user ? current_user.density|default('comfortable') : 'comfortable' }}"
      data-icon-sprite="{{ icon_sprite() }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    {#
        One page name, in one place. `heading` is what the top bar puts in the
        <h1> and what this derives the tab's title from, so the two cannot drift
        apart and a page cannot end up titled one thing and headed another. A
        page with something else to say in the tab — a subscription's own name,
        say — overrides `title` as before.
    #}
    <title>{% block title %}{{ block('heading') }} — {{ instance_name }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {#
        One stylesheet, and two kinds of URL for the scripts beside it.

        `bundle()` looks a name up in the asset build's manifest and prints the
        content-hashed file the build produced — the design tokens, Tailwind's
        theme and utilities, every rule that dresses the application, and the
        @font-face rules for the vendored Plus Jakarta Sans and JetBrains Mono. Until Phase 8 there was a
        second `<link>` here for a hand-written stylesheet served as written;
        that file moved into the build with the re-skin, because a token can
        only be the single definition of "brand" or "card" if the utility and
        the rule come out of the same compilation.

        `asset()` stamps a modification time onto the files that are still
        served exactly as written: the vendored htmx and the hand-written
        scripts. Both helpers answer the same question — what URL will a
        returning browser treat as new after an upgrade — for files that are
        produced two different ways.

        Every one of these URLs is served by this instance. Nothing on this
        page comes from a third-party host, and `assets:offline-check` fails
        the build if that stops being true.
    #}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ bundle('app.css') }}">
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/htmx.min.js') }}" defer></script>
    <script src="{{ asset('/assets/app.js') }}" defer></script>
    {# A module, so it is deferred already; `defer` on one is ignored. #}
    <script src="{{ bundle('app.js') }}" type="module"></script>
    {#
        The theme switch, on the only pages that carry one. A signed-in reader
        changes their theme under Profile → Appearance, where it is saved to
        the account, so shipping this to every page would be shipping a
        handler for a control that is never there.
    #}
    {% if not current_user and auth_chrome %}
        <script src="{{ asset('/assets/theme.js') }}" defer></script>
    {% endif %}
</head>
{#
    The CSRF token rides on every htmx request via hx-headers, so a fragment
    swap does not need a hidden field of its own.

    `has-shell` is what tells the stylesheet to leave room for the rail and the
    bottom bar. It is on the body rather than inferred from the presence of a
    sidebar, so the offset and the thing being offset around are decided by one
    condition — the same one that draws them.
#}
<body class="{{ current_user ? 'has-shell' : 'no-shell' }}"
      hx-headers='{"X-CSRF-Token": "{{ csrf_token() }}"}'>
{#
    The strings this page's JavaScript needs, from the same catalogue the
    server reads — see AppExtension::jsTranslations(). Rendered as JSON in a
    non-executing script tag rather than assigned in code, so a message
    containing a quote or a bracket cannot end up being parsed as JavaScript.
#}
<script id="renovo-messages" type="application/json">{{ js_translations()|raw }}</script>
<script>
    window.renovoI18n = (function () {
        var messages = {};

        try {
            messages = JSON.parse(document.getElementById('renovo-messages').textContent);
        } catch (error) {
            messages = {};
        }

        return {
            /* Named arguments only: the browser-side strings take no plurals. */
            t: function (key, params) {
                var message = messages[key] || key;

                if (params) {
                    Object.keys(params).forEach(function (name) {
                        message = message.split('{' + name + '}').join(params[name]);
                    });
                }

                return message;
            }
        };
    }());
</script>

<a class="skip-link" href="#main">{{ t('nav.skip_to_content') }}</a>

{#
    The mark, once per document, for whichever of the two headers below draws
    it. It used to sit inside the signed-in branch, which left `#renovo-mark`
    undefined on exactly the screens — signing in, resetting a password, the
    first-run wizard — that now want to show it.
#}
{% include 'partials/brand_sprite.twig' %}

{#
    The shell, for a signed-in page: the rail, the top bar over the content and
    the bottom bar a narrow screen gets instead of the rail. Which item each of
    them marks as current is decided here, once, from the request path — so the
    highlight is right in the HTML that arrives rather than being corrected by
    script after the page has painted.

    Sign-in, registration and the setup wizard get none of it. There is nowhere
    to navigate to before there is an account — so what they get instead is the
    branded header below, which names the instance and nothing else.

    `auth_chrome` is how the third case opts out. An error page is rendered
    with no user whoever is reading it — HttpErrorHandler cannot know, since
    the failure may have happened before the account was loaded — so without
    the flag a signed-in reader's 404 would offer them the sign-in screen's
    theme switch, a control writing a cookie that none of their pages read.
#}
{% if current_user %}
    {% set nav = navigation(scope, current_path) %}
    {#
        The shell's three reads — the household label, the Subscriptions
        badge, the bell's dot — and the rates chip, assembled once for the
        rail, the top bar and the tab bar to share. See ShellService.
    #}
    {% set shell = scope ? shell(scope) : null %}

    {% include 'partials/rail.twig' %}

    <header class="topbar">
        <div class="topbar-lead">
            {# The mark, for the widths where the rail that usually carries it is gone. #}
            <a class="topbar-brand" href="/" aria-label="{{ instance_name }}">
                <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="0 0 460 427" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <use href="#renovo-mark"></use>
                </svg>
            </a>

            <div class="topbar-heading">
                <h1 class="topbar-title">{% block heading %}{{ instance_name }}{% endblock %}</h1>

                {#
                    A short line under the title, catalogued and static. A
                    subtitle that carries figures ("12 tracked · £182/mo")
                    belongs to the page's own rebuild, where the per-currency
                    rule can be kept; this phase gives each page words only.
                    Hidden below 900px, where the title needs the room.
                #}
                {% if block('page_subtitle') is defined %}
                    <p class="topbar-subtitle">{{ block('page_subtitle') }}</p>
                {% endif %}
            </div>

            {#
                What this particular page offers: editing the thing being
                viewed, going back to a list. The one action every page offers —
                adding a subscription — is in the actions beside it and is not
                repeated here.
            #}
            <div class="topbar-page-actions">{% block page_actions %}{% endblock %}</div>
        </div>

        {% include 'partials/topbar.twig' %}
    </header>
{% elseif auth_chrome %}
    {#
        What a page rendered before there is an account gets instead of the
        shell: the mark and the instance's own name above the card.

        The name is text rather than the logo's wordmark for the reason
        `partials/brand_sprite.twig` gives — the wordmark is navy, and this
        page is as likely to be near-black as white — and because the operator
        may have named the instance something other than Renovo, in which case
        their name is the one that belongs over their sign-in form.
    #}
    <header class="auth-header">
        <svg class="brand-mark auth-mark" viewBox="0 0 460 427" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <use href="#renovo-mark"></use>
        </svg>
        <p class="auth-instance">{{ instance_name }}</p>
    </header>
{% endif %}

<main class="container" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    {% include 'partials/flashes.twig' %}
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
</main>

<footer class="container footer">
    {#
        The instance's name, except on the pages whose header has just said
        it — twice on one short page is once too many. An error page has no
        such header, so it keeps the name it always carried.
    #}
    {% if current_user or not auth_chrome %}
        <span>{{ instance_name }}</span>
    {% endif %}

    {% if current_user %}
        <button type="button" class="button-link" data-opens-dialog="shortcut-help">
            {{ t('shortcuts.title') }}
        </button>
    {% elseif auth_chrome %}
        {#
            The theme switch: three states, one of which is "whatever this
            machine asks for", so it is a group of three buttons rather than a
            two-state toggle that cannot express the default.

            `aria-pressed` is rendered by the server from the same value the
            `<html>` attribute carries, so the control states the truth before
            any script runs. Without a script it does nothing and the page is
            still themed — this changes how the page looks, not what it can do.
        #}
        <div class="segmented" role="group" aria-label="{{ t('settings.theme') }}"
             data-theme-switch>
            {% for choice, label in {
                'system': 'theme.system_short',
                'light': 'theme.light',
                'dark': 'theme.dark'
            } %}
                <button type="button" class="segmented-option" data-theme-choice="{{ choice }}"
                        aria-pressed="{{ (guest_theme|default('system')) == choice ? 'true' : 'false' }}">
                    {{ t(label) }}
                </button>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</footer>

{% if current_user %}
    {% include 'partials/tabbar.twig' %}
    {% include 'partials/dialogs.twig' %}
{% endif %}
</body>
</html>
